  2. Finish the release process already specified in docs/READINESS.md:58 and
     docs/RELEASING.md:9.
      - Select a license; add LICENSE, a reviewed third-party NOTICE, and
        SECURITY.md.

      - Restore and verify remote CI for the exact release commit; current
        local workflow configuration exists, but current docs correctly do not
        treat it as execution evidence.

      - Make a signed/tagged Go module release the primary product. Publish
        wasm demo bundles with SHA-256 checksums; do not pretend a generic
        desktop example binary is the engine product.

      - Add a manual workflow_dispatch release workflow that builds only a
        reviewed tag and has no publish permission by default.

      - Add go mod verify, govulncheck ./..., and npm audit --omit=dev
        --audit-level=high to CI. Go’s own vulnerability tooling follows
        reachable calls, making it a useful low-noise check for this
        repository. Go vulnerability management
        (https://go.dev/doc/security/vuln/)

      - SHA-pin GitHub Actions and give jobs explicit least-privilege
        permissions. GitHub recommends full commit SHAs as the immutable action
        reference. GitHub secure-use guidance
        (https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/security/secure-use)

      - If repository eligibility permits it, attach binary and SBOM provenance
        to release artifacts. GitHub supports attestations for binaries and
        SBOMs, but private repositories require Enterprise Cloud. GitHub
        artifact attestations
        (https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/secure-your-work/use-artifact-attestations/use-artifact-attestations)

  3. Treat platform support as a testable product feature.
      - Certify Windows and macOS on real hardware or change their wording from
        target platforms to build-only targets.

      - Run Linux X11, Windows, macOS, Chromium, Firefox, and Safari tests
        where those platforms are claimed. Record resize, DPI, minimize/
        restore, user close, texture upload, device loss/recovery, and clean
        shutdown.

      - Keep browser WebGPU experimental for v0.1 unless this matrix passes.
        WebGPU still has limited browser availability and requires HTTPS/secure
        contexts. MDN WebGPU
        (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebGPU_API)

      - Do not add a WebGL fallback yet. First decide whether 72’s browser
        product is “WebGPU-only experimental” or “broadly deployable web
        games.” The latter requires a deliberately supported fallback backend,
        not a quick compatibility layer.

      - Exercise device loss explicitly; the WebGPU standard exposes this
        through GPUDevice.lost, so it is a real lifecycle state, not just an
        error-path curiosity. WebGPU device-loss specification
        (https://www.w3.org/TR/webgpu/#device-loss)

